  marginal costs $/MWh: solar=0.50, solar=0.50, solar=0.50, solar=0.50, solar=0.50, solar=0.51, wind=1.51, wind=1.51, wind=1.51, wind=1.51, wind=1.51, wind=1.51, BIT (Bituminous Coal)=23.27, NG (Natural Gas)=22.46, NUC (Nuclear)=13.70, NG (Natural Gas)=21.22, NG (Natural Gas)=22.10, NUC (Nuclear)=15.39
  NOTE 270 of 280 thermal heat rates were floored to physical minima (the dataset's gas heat rates are not physical)

==============================================================================
E1  The price of the missing insurance market
==============================================================================
  Without Arrow-Debreu securities Eq. (94) fails and the risk-aware
  allocation cannot be decentralised. At eps = 0 every agent's gamma is
  zero, so (94) holds trivially: an energy-only market DOES support the
  risk-neutral allocation. The risk-adjusted welfare difference between
  the two is the value of completing the market.

  risk-adjusted loss rho_RA[l]  (eps=0.3, beta=0.95):
    risk-neutral commitment (energy-only market) :     -24160418.00 $/h
    risk-aware commitment   (with insurance)     :     -24193203.79 $/h
    VALUE OF THE INSURANCE MARKET                :         32785.79 $/h   (0.1357% of risk-adjusted welfare)
    per MWh served (26,818 MWh/h)          :           1.2225 $/MWh
    annualised (x8760)                           : $287.20 M/yr

  decomposition (loss, so lower is better):
    E[l]        risk-neutral   -26223388.33   risk-aware   -26174392.41   delta    +48995.92
    CVaR_b[l]   risk-neutral   -19346820.54   risk-aware   -19570430.33   delta   -223609.79
    (risk-aware clearing gives up expected welfare to cut the tail;
     the insurance market is what makes that trade decentralisable.)

  Eq. (94) without insurance, on this fleet:
    planner tail scenarios: 26 of 500
    agents' tail scenarios: min 25, max 25  -> the agents do not agree on where the tail is
    max_k |gamma_k - gamma| = 0.012000   (Eq. 94 requires 0)

==============================================================================
E2  Risk incidence by generation technology
==============================================================================
  At equilibrium the insurance price is q = rho_tilde, so the premium an
  agent pays in excess of its expected payout is sum_w (q_w - rho_w) r_w.
  Under the corrected Eq. (111)-(112), r_k = L_k - l/N - delta_k, and that
  splits exactly into
      OWN RISK   pi_k := sum_w (q_w - rho_w) L_kw     (agent-specific)
      POOL SHARE       -(1/N) sum_w (q_w - rho_w) l_w (identical for all)
  The delta_k drop out of both. pi_k is the economically meaningful term:
  the equilibrium price of the tail risk that agent k's OWN profit stream
  brings to the market. The pool share is a flat rebate that the
  equal-share rule hands every agent regardless of contribution.

  Eq.(95) clearing check: max_w |sum_k r_k(w)| = 1.118e-08

  flat pool share handed to every agent: -104,273.09 $/h

  technology                 n   own risk pi_k $/h   $/MW capacity   equal-share net
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  solar                      6           -2,787.27          -1.427       -628,425.78
  wind                       6           -8,529.33          -1.871       -634,167.84
  BIT (Bituminous Coal)      1                0.00           0.000       -104,273.09
  NG (Natural Gas)           3           -6,206.66          -0.340       -319,025.91
  NUC (Nuclear)              2                0.00           0.000       -208,546.17
  LOAD                       1        1,998,711.88          74.529      1,894,438.79
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  RENEWABLE (solar+wind)                -11,316.60          -1.738
  THERMAL                                -6,206.66          -0.169
  LOAD                                1,998,711.88

  share of total |tail-risk premium|  (renewable share 15.1%):
    renewables   0.6%    thermal   0.3%    load  99.1%
  The load is the residual risk bearer: pi_LOAD > 0 means its loss spikes
  exactly in the scenarios the market prices most heavily, so it is the
  natural BUYER of cover. Renewables are mildly negative (they earn more
  in scarcity when they do produce), so they SELL cover.
  pi is EXACTLY zero for coal and nuclear, and that is a property of the
  dataset, not of the theory: thermal capacity in thermal_gens.csv is
  constant across scenarios, so an infra-marginal unit has a constant L
  and sum_w (q_w - rho_w) L = L * 0 = 0. Modelling forced outages would
  give thermal genuine tail exposure; we flag this rather than hide it.
  NOTE the equal-share column is nearly identical across generators: the
  flat pool share swamps the signal. That is a property of the Eq. (111)
  rule, not of the market, and is worth saying in the paper -- a
  contribution-weighted delta_k would allocate the rebate sensibly.

==============================================================================
E3  Decarbonisation sweep: renewable share at FIXED total capacity
==============================================================================
  Adding renewables on top of an unchanged thermal fleet does not change
  scarcity risk -- firm capacity still covers peak load, so the tail is
  untouched and the value of insurance is flat. (We checked; it is.)
  Decarbonisation is a SUBSTITUTION: thermal retires as renewables enter.
  So we hold total installed capacity and the load fixed, and vary the
  mix. Firm capacity then falls as the renewable share rises, which is
  what actually puts weight in the tail.

  total installed capacity held at 43,243 MW; load level frozen at scale 0.2363

   target  ren.share    firm MW  ins. value $/h    $/MWh    E[l] delta    CVaR delta  ren. own risk
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     0.15      0.150     36,757       32,789.80   1.2227        48,997      -223,625     -1,262,568
     0.30      0.300     30,270       33,323.39   1.2426        49,323      -226,164     -1,271,275
     0.45      0.450     23,784       33,186.56   1.2375        48,711      -224,280     -1,279,745
     0.60      0.600     17,297       32,599.74   1.2156        47,540      -219,593     -1,323,914
     0.75      0.750     10,811       31,372.06   1.1698        41,586      -201,608     -1,355,288

  from 15.0% to 75.0% renewable share, the value of the insurance market per MWh moves from 1.2227 to 1.1698 $/MWh (factor 0.96).

==============================================================================
E6  Sensitivity to the deviation bid price (and why it must be reported)
==============================================================================
  The planner's loss charges alpha^+/alpha^- for deviating from the
  day-ahead schedule (paper Eq. 3). In the code these were hard-coded as
  mc/10 and VOLL/10 with the comment 'just a heuristic'. They are not a
  harmless heuristic: the value of risk-aware clearing is almost exactly
  LINEAR in them, so any single headline $/MWh figure is a restatement of
  that constant. Reporting the slope instead makes the result parameter-free.

   delta_scale  alpha_load $/MWh  ins. value $/h     $/MWh  $/MWh per unit delta
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
         0.020              20.0           6,557    0.2445               12.2253
         0.050              50.0          16,393    0.6113               12.2253
         0.100             100.0          32,786    1.2225               12.2253
         0.200             200.0          65,572    2.4451               12.2253
         0.400             400.0         131,143    4.8901               12.2253

  slope is constant to within 0.000%  =>  value of the insurance market = 12.225 x delta_scale  $/MWh
  equivalently 1.2225% of the load's deviation
  bid price per MWh served. THIS is the number the paper should quote:
  it is a property of the uncertainty, not of an arbitrary constant.

